 "reviewHtml": "<div><p>Francesco Murano went to <em>Metropolis</em> for his starting point (a particularly timely choice, given that Fritz Lang’s 1927 vision is set in 2026), and to the film’s two Marias for his central dichotomy: the virtuous woman and her seductive robotic double. His response, a collection called Éidola, which is Ancient Greek for ‘image,’ took the two and, sensibly, refused to choose.</p><p>The more interesting story, however, was what Murano does with fabric. Draping remains his strongest language, and here it was pushed towards a new architectural precision. Glossy jersey was folded into rigid, radiating pleats that gradually dissolved into fluidity, giving the lightness of the fabric an almost armored structure. Other dresses were reduced to fluttering geometric panels, sometimes entirely without seams, their fit created by folds rather than construction. They looked deceptively simple, which is always a dangerous word in fashion, because the simpler the surface, the more mercilessly it exposes the hand behind it.</p><p>The silhouette moved between sculptural verticality and softer, more sensual shapes, with capes wrapping the shoulders and arms before opening dramatically at the back. A new embroidery of metal studs on georgette mimicked chain mail, turning protection into something fluid and erotic. Even Murano’s familiar sensuality came with a certain discipline: the body was revealed in less predictable places, rather than through the usual inventory of fashion’s favorite erogenous zones.</p><p>The references to Expressionism, Art Deco and the warrior goddess were there, but what stayed with you was the technique. Murano is becoming increasingly adept at turning draping into architecture, and architecture back into movement: a more convincing kind of futurism than any robot woman could provide.</p></div>",
